xnu 0372: free the blocks a delete actually owns

Measured 2026-11-19: deleting a 1 MiB file (`/private/var/vm/mondrian-fill`) moved
`statfs` from 0 to 24 free blocks, not 256. `BlockMarkFreeInternal` abandoned the
whole range at the first word that was not entirely allocated and returned
`hfsNotAllocatedErr`, and `BlockDeallocate` skipped the counter on that error. The
rest of the range stayed marked allocated in the bitmap and was lost: every log
rotation leaks the blocks it just wrote, which is why the volume fell from 164 MB
free to zero inside one session.

The free path now clears the bits that are set, skips the ones that are already
clear, and counts what it cleared. `BlockDeallocate` adds that count to
`freeBlocks` (and to `hfs_freed_block_count`) instead of the requested range, so
the counter and the bitmap stay in step. A range that held nothing but free
blocks still answers `hfsNotAllocatedErr`, which is the signal the callers act on
and the case the old comment describes.

That is also the 32 MB the audit measured between the counter and the bitmap: the
same defect, over the ranges freed since the image was written.

